<PaperLayout
  title="BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers"
  eyebrow="NLP · BERT · 2018 / NAACL"
  authors="Jacob Devlin · Ming-Wei Chang · Kenton Lee · Kristina Toutanova"
  arxivUrl="https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805"
  subtitle="A structured reading of the paper that turned the Transformer encoder into a general-purpose language-understanding engine."
  sections={sections}
  annotations={annotations}
  initialAnnotation="bert"
  compactTitle={true}
>
        <section id="overview" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Overview</h2>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>Reading guide</strong>
            <p>
              Read BERT as a paper about <em>how to pretrain understanding</em>. The Transformer paper showed
              that attention could replace recurrence for sequence modeling. BERT asks a different question:
              if a Transformer encoder can let every token look at every other token, can we pretrain that
              encoder once and reuse it across many language-understanding tasks?
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            <em>BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding</em> introduces
            <button class="anno" data-anno="bert">BERT</button>, an encoder-only Transformer pretrained on
            ordinary text and then adapted to different <button class="anno" data-anno="downstream">downstream tasks</button>.
            Its headline idea is simple: build a model whose token representations are deeply
            <button class="anno" data-anno="bidirectional">bidirectional</button>, then make the same pretrained
            model useful for classification, inference, question answering, and token labeling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important word is <strong>representation</strong>. BERT is not mainly a text generator. It is a
            model that turns tokens into contextual vectors: the representation of a word depends on the whole
            sentence, the sentence pair, and the task-specific fine-tuning signal. That is why the paper matters
            historically. It helped move NLP from hand-building a new architecture for every benchmark toward
            reusing a large pretrained model as the starting point for many tasks.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>Unlabeled text</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>MLM + NSP pre-training</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Pretrained BERT</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Task-specific fine-tuning</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="why" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Why BERT appeared</h2>

          <p>
            BERT appears at a specific moment in NLP history. Researchers already knew that pretraining on
            large text corpora helped. Static word embeddings made downstream systems better; ELMo made word
            representations depend on context; OpenAI GPT showed that a Transformer could be pretrained and
            then fine-tuned. But the field still had an unresolved tension: the best fine-tuning recipe was
            <button class="anno" data-anno="unidirectional">unidirectional</button>, while many understanding
            tasks clearly require both left and right context.
          </p>

          <p>
            A left-to-right <button class="anno" data-anno="causalLM">Causal Language Model</button> is natural
            for generation: predict the next token from previous tokens. But language understanding often works
            differently. In question answering, a candidate answer token may need right-side context to determine
            its boundary. In natural language inference, one sentence must be interpreted against another. In
            named entity recognition, a token's type can depend on words before and after it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The obvious solution, "let every token see both sides," creates a training problem. If the model is
            asked to predict a word while the original word is still visible in the input, it can cheat. BERT's
            central trick is to change the objective: hide some tokens first, then predict them. This is the
            <button class="anno" data-anno="mlm">Masked Language Model</button>. The architecture can be fully
            bidirectional because the target token itself is removed or corrupted.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The gap BERT fills</span>
            <p>
              ELMo had context but was mostly <button class="anno" data-anno="featureBased">feature-based</button>. GPT had <button class="anno" data-anno="fineTuning">fine-tuning</button> but was left-to-right. BERT
              combines the useful parts: a Transformer fine-tuning framework, plus deeply bidirectional
              representations trained with a self-supervised objective.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="related" class="reading-section">
          <h2>The road to BERT</h2>

          <p>
            BERT is built by looking at what earlier representation-learning methods did well, then asking which limitations
            still prevented a single pretrained model from serving as a general language-understanding backbone.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Line of work</th>
                  <th>Examples</th>
                  <th>What it contributed</th>
                  <th>What BERT changes</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {relatedRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.line}</td>
                    <td>{row.examples}</td>
                    <td>{row.contribution}</td>
                    <td>{row.limitation}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The closest contrast is between <button class="anno" data-anno="elmo">ELMo</button>,
            <button class="anno" data-anno="gpt">OpenAI GPT</button>, and BERT. ELMo trains a left-to-right
            model and a right-to-left model, then concatenates the resulting features. GPT trains a Transformer
            with left-to-right language modeling, then fine-tunes. BERT trains one
            <button class="anno" data-anno="transformerEncoder">Transformer encoder</button> whose layers can
            jointly condition on both directions from the beginning.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            {representationRouteRows.map((item) => (
              <div class="concept-card">
                <h3>{item.name}</h3>
                <p>{item.text}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="core" class="reading-section">
          <h2>The core idea</h2>

          <p>
            BERT's core idea is not merely "make a bigger Transformer." It is the alignment of three choices:
            an encoder architecture, a bidirectional pre-training objective, and a fine-tuning interface that
            can express many NLP tasks with small task heads.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow">
            <span>Transformer encoder</span>
            <span>+</span>
            <span>Masked token prediction</span>
            <span>+</span>
            <span>Sentence-pair prediction</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Reusable language representation</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            The subtle point is that BERT's bidirectionality is legal because the target is hidden. A normal
            language model cannot freely look left and right while predicting a visible word. BERT can, because
            it first replaces selected words with <code>[MASK]</code>, random words, or leaves them unchanged as
            prediction targets. The model is trained to recover the original token, not to generate the next token
            in a left-to-right stream.
          </p>

          <p>
            This makes BERT especially natural for tasks where the answer is a representation, a class, or a span
            inside the input. It is less natural for open-ended generation than GPT-style models, but extremely
            powerful for language understanding, ranking, extraction, and classification.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="architecture" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Architecture</h2>

          <p>
            BERT's architecture is a stack of Transformer encoder blocks. The paper describes the model size
            with three symbols: <strong>L</strong> is the number of Transformer layers,
            <strong>H</strong> is the hidden size of each token representation, and
            <strong>A</strong> is the number of self-attention heads. So when the paper says BERTBASE has
            L=12, H=768, and A=12, it means every token is represented as a 768-dimensional vector through
            a 12-layer encoder stack with 12 attention heads per layer.
          </p>

