          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>Why BERT can be bidirectional</strong>
            <p>
              A normal left-to-right language model cannot simply look at both sides when predicting a token,
              because the original token would still be visible in the input. BERT solves this by changing the
              pre-training problem. It first hides or corrupts selected tokens, then asks the encoder to recover
              the original tokens. The architecture can attend bidirectionally because the answer token itself is
              no longer directly available.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>One encoder, different task heads</h3>

          <p>
            The same pretrained BERT encoder can be reused for different downstream tasks. What changes is not
            the main encoder stack, but the input format and the small output layer. This is the practical reason
            BERT felt so powerful: many tasks could be reframed as "put the right sequence into BERT, then read
            the right output position."
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>Input tokens</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>BERT encoder</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>[CLS] vector or token vectors</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>task head</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            This section is where BERT's "encoder-only" identity matters. GPT-style models are built for
            autoregressive generation, so their attention is causally masked. BERT removes that causal mask,
            uses a Transformer encoder, and relies on masked prediction to keep pre-training valid.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="representations" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Input representation</h2>

          <p>
            BERT's input format is one of the paper's most practical contributions. It creates a single sequence
            format that can represent one sentence, two sentences, a question-passage pair, or a multiple-choice
            candidate. Every input starts with <button class="anno" data-anno="cls">[CLS]</button>, and text spans
            are separated by <button class="anno" data-anno="sep">[SEP]</button>.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>input vector = token embedding + segment embedding + position embedding</code>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are added rather than concatenated because all three vectors have the same hidden dimension
            <code>H</code>. For BERTBASE, each one is 768-dimensional, and their sum is still 768-dimensional.
            The model learns the scale and use of each component during training. In plain language:
            <button class="anno" data-anno="tokenEmbedding">token embeddings</button> say what the token is,
            <button class="anno" data-anno="segmentEmbedding">segment embeddings</button> say which text span it
            belongs to, and <button class="anno" data-anno="positionEmbedding">position embeddings</button> say
            where it appears.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow">
            <span>[CLS]</span>
            <span>my dog is cute</span>
            <span>[SEP]</span>
            <span>he likes play ##ing</span>
            <span>[SEP]</span>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Surface token</th>
                  <th>WordPiece</th>
                  <th>Segment</th>
                  <th>Position</th>
                  <th>Role</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {representationRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.token}</td>
                    <td>{row.wordpiece}</td>
                    <td>{row.segment}</td>
                    <td>{row.position}</td>
                    <td>{row.role}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that <button class="anno" data-anno="wordpiece">WordPiece</button> tokenization can split a
            single word into multiple subword tokens. BERT predicts and represents WordPiece tokens, not always
            whole human-written words. This makes the vocabulary manageable while still covering rare or novel words.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="pretraining" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Pre-training objectives</h2>

          <p>
            BERT is pretrained on unlabeled text, but that does not mean it has no training targets. The targets
            are created automatically from the text itself. This is why BERT's pre-training is better described
            as <button class="anno" data-anno="selfSupervision">self-supervision</button>: ordinary text is
            converted into prediction problems before any human-labeled downstream dataset is used.
          </p>

          <h3>The pre-training to fine-tuning pipeline</h3>

          <p>
            BERT follows a two-stage recipe: first train a general encoder with self-supervised objectives,
            then copy the same pretrained parameters into different downstream models. The architecture is almost
            the same on both sides; the task-specific output layer changes.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>BooksCorpus + Wikipedia</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>MLM + NSP</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Pretrained BERT</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Fine-tuned task models</span>
          </div>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>The key distinction</strong>
            <p>
              Pre-training learns a general initialization from large text. Fine-tuning creates separate
              task-specific models from that same initialization. In the original BERT setup, MNLI, SQuAD,
              SWAG, and NER do not share one final multitask model; each task gets its own fine-tuned copy.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Objective</th>
                  <th>Example input</th>
                  <th>Training target</th>
                  <th>Why it exists</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {pretrainingRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.objective}</td>
                    <td><code>{row.input}</code></td>
                    <td>{row.target}</td>
                    <td>{row.reason}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>Masked Language Model</h3>

          <p>
            The <button class="anno" data-anno="mlm">Masked Language Model</button> is BERT's answer to the
            bidirectionality problem. A standard language model predicts from one direction to avoid seeing the
            target token. BERT instead corrupts some input positions first. The encoder can then look left and
            right, because the original target token has been removed or changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            BERT selects 15% of WordPiece tokens as prediction targets. Those selected targets are handled with
            the 80/10/10 masking strategy below. Notice that the model's target is always the original token,
            even when the input token is unchanged.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Selected target case</th>
                  <th>Input shown to BERT</th>
                  <th>Prediction target</th>
                  <th>Purpose</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {maskRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.case}</td>
                    <td><code>{row.input}</code></td>
                    <td><code>{row.target}</code></td>
                    <td>{row.purpose}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>Next Sentence Prediction</h3>

          <p>
            <button class="anno" data-anno="nsp">Next Sentence Prediction</button> gives BERT a sentence-pair
            pre-training signal. For each pair, sentence B is the true next sentence 50% of the time and a random
            sentence 50% of the time. The final hidden state of <button class="anno" data-anno="cls">[CLS]</button>
            is used to predict <strong>IsNext</strong> or <strong>NotNext</strong>.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>[CLS] sentence A [SEP] sentence B [SEP]</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>BERT encoder</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>[CLS]</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>IsNext / NotNext</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            NSP is most naturally connected to text-pair tasks. Question answering, natural language inference,
            and paraphrase detection all require the model to represent relationships between two pieces of text.
            Later models debated whether NSP was the best way to learn this signal, but in the original BERT
            paper it is part of the main recipe and is tested in the ablation studies.
          </p>

          <h3>Pre-training loss</h3>

          <p>
            BERT's pre-training loss has two parts: a token-level MLM loss and a sequence-level NSP loss. The
            paper uses the sum of these losses. Conceptually, the model is punished when it fails to reconstruct
            selected original tokens, and also when it fails to classify whether the second text segment is the
            real next segment.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <div class="formula-label">Total pre-training loss</div>

            <div class="formula-line">
              <span>L<sub>BERT</sub></span>
              <span>=</span>
              <span>L<sub>MLM</sub></span>
              <span>+</span>
              <span>L<sub>NSP</sub></span>
            </div>

            <p class="formula-caption">
              BERT is trained with two losses at the same time: one for reconstructing selected tokens,
              and one for judging whether two text segments are consecutive.
            </p>

            <p class="formula-note">
              <strong>L<sub>MLM</sub></strong> is computed only over the selected 15% prediction positions.
              <strong>L<sub>NSP</sub></strong> is computed from the binary IsNext / NotNext classifier using
              the <code>[CLS]</code> representation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Loss part</th>
                  <th>Where the signal comes from</th>
                  <th>Target</th>
                  <th>Role in pre-training</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {lossRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.part}</td>
                    <td>{row.signal}</td>
                    <td>{row.target}</td>
                    <td>{row.role}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The MLM loss is not computed for every token in the sequence. It is computed only at the selected
            prediction positions, which are 15% of WordPiece tokens. For each selected position, BERT takes the
            final hidden vector at that position and predicts a distribution over the vocabulary. The correct
            class is the original token id. This is the same cross-entropy idea used in many classification
            problems, but here the classes are vocabulary items.
          </p>

          <p>
            The NSP loss is a binary cross-entropy classification loss. The input pair is encoded by BERT, the
            <code>[CLS]</code> vector is read as the sequence-pair representation, and the classifier predicts
            whether the pair is <strong>IsNext</strong> or <strong>NotNext</strong>. The final pre-training update
            combines this sentence-pair loss with the masked-token reconstruction loss.
          </p>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>Why this loss design matters</strong>
            <p>
              MLM makes deep bidirectional pre-training possible. NSP adds a sentence-pair signal. Together they
              let BERT learn both local token meaning and broader text-pair relationships before any supervised
              downstream fine-tuning.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="finetuning" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Fine-tuning</h2>

          <p>
            BERT's downstream recipe is deliberately plain. Start from the same pretrained checkpoint, add a
            small task-specific output layer, and train all parameters on the labeled dataset.
            This is the fine-tuning approach, not just feature extraction.
            Each downstream task gets a separate fine-tuned model, but they all begin from the same pretrained BERT.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Task type</th>
                  <th>BERT input</th>
                  <th>Output</th>
                  <th>Example</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {fineTuningRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.task}</td>
                    <td><code>{row.input}</code></td>
                    <td>{row.output}</td>
                    <td>{row.example}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most elegant case is extractive question answering. BERT reads the question and passage as one
            packed sequence. Then the task head scores each passage token as a possible start and a possible end.
            For SQuAD v2.0, where some questions have no answer, BERT treats the <code>[CLS]</code> position as
            the no-answer span and compares that score against the best non-null span.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why BERT reduced the need for heavily engineered task architectures. Earlier systems often
            designed separate cross-attention modules for each task. BERT lets the self-attention inside the packed
            input perform much of that interaction automatically.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="tasks" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Tasks and benchmarks</h2>

          <p>
            The paper's benchmarks are not random. They cover sentence-level classification, sentence-pair
            reasoning, token-level extraction, and commonsense-style multiple choice. This breadth is the evidence
            behind the claim that BERT learns general-purpose language representations.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Dataset / benchmark</th>
                  <th>Task</th>
                  <th>Input</th>
                  <th>Output</th>
                  <th>Example</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {benchmarkRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.name}</td>
                    <td>{row.task}</td>
                    <td>{row.input}</td>
                    <td>{row.output}</td>
                    <td>{row.example}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Some names are especially important for reading the paper. <button class="anno" data-anno="nli">Natural
            Language Inference</button> asks whether one sentence follows from another. <button class="anno" data-anno="paraphrasing">Paraphrasing</button>
            asks whether two texts mean the same thing. <button class="anno" data-anno="ner">Named Entity Recognition</button>
            assigns labels to tokens. <button class="anno" data-anno="qa">Question Answering</button> asks BERT to
            find answer spans. Together, these tasks test very different uses of the same representation backbone.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="experiments" class="reading-section">
          <h2>What the experiments prove</h2>

          <p>
            The experiments are best read as a proof of the BERT recipe. The point is not simply that the tables
            contain high numbers. The point is that a single architecture pattern works across very different
            task formats with only small output changes.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Experiment area</th>
                  <th>Main result</th>
                  <th>Interpretation</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {experimentRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.area}</td>
                    <td>{row.result}</td>
                    <td>{row.meaning}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            <button class="anno" data-anno="glue">GLUE</button> is the broad test: BERT improves tasks involving
            inference, similarity, sentiment, paraphrase, and grammatical acceptability. <button class="anno" data-anno="squad">SQuAD</button>
            is the token-level test: the model must identify exact spans, not just classify whole sentences.
            <button class="anno" data-anno="swag">SWAG</button> is the multiple-choice continuation test: the model
            must score candidate endings against a context.
          </p>

          <p>
            The comparison with GPT is especially revealing. BERTBASE was intentionally sized close to OpenAI GPT,
            so the improvement cannot be explained only by saying "BERT is bigger." The paper argues that the
            key difference is the attention mask and objective: GPT is left-to-right; BERT is bidirectional through MLM.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="ablation" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Ablation studies</h2>

          <p>
            The ablations ask what part of the recipe actually matters. If BERT only worked because of more data
            or more parameters, removing NSP or replacing MLM with left-to-right language modeling would not be so
            informative. But the ablation results show clear patterns.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Variant</th>
                  <th>What changes</th>
                  <th>Takeaway</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {ablationRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.variant}</td>
                    <td>{row.change}</td>
                    <td>{row.takeaway}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most important ablation is the left-to-right variant. It performs much worse on SQuAD, which is
            exactly where the theory predicts trouble: token-level answer representations need right context.
            Adding a randomly initialized BiLSTM helps SQuAD but does not catch up, because it is not the same as
            pretraining every layer with bidirectional context from the start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The model-size ablation also mattered historically. It showed that once a model is sufficiently
            pretrained, larger encoders can help even on small downstream datasets. This was one early sign that
            scale and pretraining could change the usual fear that large models simply overfit small NLP tasks.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="appendix" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Implementation details</h2>

          <p>
            BERT's main idea is simple, but the implementation recipe matters. The model is not just a
            bidirectional encoder in the abstract; it is trained with a specific data construction process,
            masking strategy, sequence length schedule, and fine-tuning setup. These details make the method
            reproducible and help explain why the original results were strong.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Detail</th>
                  <th>Value / explanation</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {appendixRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.item}</td>
                    <td>{row.value}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most important implementation detail is that BERT does not predict every token. It selects a
            small subset of WordPiece tokens as prediction targets, corrupts some of them, and computes the MLM
            loss only at those selected positions. This makes MLM less prediction-dense than a left-to-right
            language model, but it gives the model something more valuable for understanding: every selected
            token can be recovered using both left and right context.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sequence-length schedule also matters. Full self-attention becomes expensive as the sequence
            gets longer, so BERT is mostly pretrained with shorter sequences and only later trained with the
            full 512-token length. This keeps pre-training more efficient while still teaching the model how to
            use long-range position embeddings.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">A useful way to read the recipe</span>
            <p>
              The architecture gives BERT the capacity to connect every token with every other token. The
              masking procedure prevents the model from seeing the answer directly. The large text corpus gives
              it broad language exposure. Fine-tuning then turns that general representation into a task-specific
              model. The recipe works because these pieces support each other.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="impact" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Impact and afterlife</h2>

          <p>
            BERT's impact was both scientific and practical. Scientifically, it made masked encoder pre-training a
            standard baseline for language understanding. Practically, it gave NLP engineers a reusable pattern:
            take a pretrained checkpoint, convert the task into a BERT input format, add a small head, and fine-tune.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Period</th>
                  <th>What changed</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {timelineRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.year}</td>
                    <td>{row.event}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            BERT also clarified a split that still matters. Encoder models are excellent when the goal is to
            understand, classify, rank, embed, retrieve, or extract from given text. Decoder-only models are better
            suited to open-ended generation. Modern systems often combine these families: a BERT-like encoder or
            embedding model may retrieve and rank evidence, while a GPT-like decoder writes the final response.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="commercial" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Why GPT became more visible</h2>

          <p>
            A natural question after reading BERT is: if BERT was so important, why do most people hear about
            GPT products instead of BERT products? The short answer is that <strong>generation is easier to
            package as a consumer product, while understanding is usually hidden inside infrastructure</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            GPT-style models are trained to continue text from left to right. That objective maps directly onto
            products people can see: chatbots, writing assistants, coding assistants, copilots, and agents. A user
            types a prompt, and the model produces a visible answer. The model's core ability is already the
            interface.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>User prompt</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>GPT-like decoder</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Generated answer</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            BERT-style models have a different product shape. BERT reads a piece of text and turns it into a
            contextual representation. That representation is useful for judging relevance, assigning labels,
            extracting spans, matching a query to a document, or ranking candidates. These are extremely valuable
            commercial functions, but they usually happen behind the scenes.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>Query or document</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>BERT-like encoder</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Vector, label, score, or span</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>System decision</span>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Model family</th>
                  <th>Core strength</th>
                  <th>Common commercial use</th>
                  <th>Product shape</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {commercialRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.family}</td>
                    <td>{row.strength}</td>
                    <td>{row.typicalUse}</td>
                    <td>{row.productShape}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is why BERT can be everywhere without feeling like a product. Search engines, recommendation
            systems, customer-support routing, content moderation, document retrieval, entity extraction, and
            reranking systems all need strong language understanding. Users usually see the final ranked result,
            label, or extracted answer, not the encoder model that helped produce it.
          </p>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>A useful mental model</strong>
            <p>
              BERT reads well. GPT writes well. That sentence is simplified, but it explains the commercial
              difference. Reading is often an internal capability inside a larger system; writing can become a
              standalone product interface.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            BERT also shaped later systems even when the product name did not include "BERT." Many later encoder
            models and embedding models inherit the same broad idea: pretrain a language model to produce useful
            representations, then use those representations for search, ranking, classification, retrieval, and
            extraction. GPT became more visible because the user can talk to it directly, but BERT's legacy lives
            in the infrastructure that helps machines understand.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="takeaways" class="reading-section final-takeaways">
          <h2>Takeaways</h2>

          <ol>
            <li>BERT is an encoder-only Transformer designed for language understanding rather than open-ended generation.</li>
            <li>Its key training idea is MLM: hide or corrupt selected tokens, then predict the originals from bidirectional context.</li>
            <li>NSP adds a sentence-pair pre-training signal for the original BERT recipe.</li>
            <li>The input representation is the sum of token, segment, and position embeddings.</li>
            <li><code>[CLS]</code> provides a sequence-level representation; <code>[SEP]</code> separates text spans.</li>
            <li>Fine-tuning means each downstream task gets its own task head and its own fine-tuned copy of the pretrained model.</li>
            <li>BERT's experiments show that one pretrained backbone can handle GLUE, SQuAD, SWAG, and token-level tasks with minimal architecture changes.</li>
            <li>The ablations show that deep bidirectional pre-training matters, especially for token-level question answering.</li>
            <li>BERT sits historically between ELMo and GPT: it keeps contextual representation and fine-tuning, but changes the directionality problem.</li>
            <li>Its afterlife includes many encoder variants and a lasting role in search, retrieval, classification, ranking, extraction, and embeddings.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>
